course: english
lesson: 27
title: Word classes: the jobs words do
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every word in a sentence has a job, and the job is its word class. In «The hungry fox quickly ate the cake under the table», fox and cake and table are nouns (naming words), ate is a verb (doing or being), hungry is an adjective (it describes the noun fox), quickly is an adverb (it tells how he ate), the is a determiner, and under is a preposition (it shows where).
explain: A word's class depends on its job in THIS sentence. Theo asks, «So run is a verb?» In «I run every day», yes. In «I went for a run», run is a noun: it comes after «a» and names a thing. In «Fish swim» fish is a noun; in «We fish on Sundays» it is a verb. Always look at the job, not just the word.
explain: Lily lists adjectives: big, red, happy, quickly. Quickly is the odd one out: it describes how something is done, so it's an adverb. Many adverbs end in -ly, but not all words ending in -ly are adverbs: friendly and lovely describe nouns («a friendly dog»), so they are adjectives. And not all adverbs end in -ly: soon, very, often and fast can be adverbs too.
explain: Pronouns stand in for nouns: «Sam said he would bring his dog», where he stands in for Sam. Conjunctions join: «I was tired but I kept going.» Proper nouns name one particular person, place or thing, so they need a capital: Ellie must write London, not london. Little words like my, your and his before a noun are determiners in school grammar (possessive determiners), while mine and yours standing alone are pronouns.
explain: Slang has word classes too. Kayla in London says, «Bare words have jobs, innit» (bare is London slang for lots of). Freya in Melbourne asks, «Arvo is a noun, yeah nah?» (arvo is Australian for afternoon, and yeah nah means no or not really). Arvo names a time of day, so it is a noun. Ethan in Ohio says, «That game was super fun»: super makes fun stronger, like very, so super is working as an adverb.
fact: A noun names a person, place, thing or idea: girl, Leeds, table, happiness.
fact: A proper noun names one particular person, place or thing and starts with a capital letter: Ellie, London, Tuesday.
fact: A verb is a doing or being word: run, eat, is, think.
fact: An adjective describes a noun: a big dog, a red hat, a friendly girl.
fact: An adverb describes a verb, an adjective or another adverb: she ran quickly, very tall, super fun.
fact: Many adverbs end in -ly, but friendly, lovely and silly are adjectives, and soon, often and very are adverbs without -ly.
fact: A pronoun stands in for a noun: he, she, it, they, we, him, her, them, mine, yours.
fact: A determiner comes before a noun to show which or how many: the, a, an, this, some, two, my, your.
fact: My, your, his, her, our and their before a noun are called possessive determiners in school grammar.
fact: A preposition shows where or when one thing is in relation to another: under, on, after, before, between.
fact: A conjunction joins words or clauses: and, but, or, so, because, when, although.
fact: The same word can belong to different classes depending on its job in the sentence.
fact: Words like before and after are prepositions when a noun follows them («before lunch») and adverbs when they stand alone («I'd seen it before»).
fact: Slang: «bare» (London) means lots of; «arvo» (Australia) means afternoon; «yeah nah» (Australia, New Zealand) means no or not really; «well» before a describing word (Britain) means very, as in «well good».
exercise: In «I went for a run», what word class is «run»? | A noun | A verb, because run is always a doing word | An adjective | A preposition
exercise: Lily lists big, red, happy and quickly as adjectives. Which is the odd one out? | Quickly, it's an adverb | Big, because it's a size and not a feeling | Red, because colours are nouns | Happy, because it's a feeling
exercise: «Sam said he would bring his dog.» Which word is a pronoun? | he | Sam | dog | bring
exercise: Kayla is asked to name the word class of «the». What is it? | A determiner | A pronoun | A noun | A verb
exercise: In «under the bed», what word class is «under»? | A preposition | An adverb, because it tells you where something is | A noun | A conjunction
exercise: «I was tired but I kept going.» Find the conjunction. | but | tired | kept | going
exercise: Ellie writes «london». What must change, and why? | London, because it's a proper noun | Nothing, place names are written small | LONDON, because places are shouted | London, because it is a verb
exercise: Ethan says, «That game was super fun.» Which word is working as an adverb? | super | game | was | fun
exercise: What word class is «happily»? | An adverb | An adjective | A noun | A verb
exercise: Ahmed asks if «my» in «my dog» is a pronoun. What does his teacher say? | It's a possessive determiner | It's a noun, because it names the person who owns the dog | It's a verb | It's a conjunction
exercise: Freya asks if arvo is a noun. Is it? | Yes, it names a time of day | No, it's a verb meaning to arrive | No, it's an adjective | No, slang words have no word class
exercise: Kayla says «bare words». What does bare mean in London slang? | Lots of | No | Naked | Really cold
exercise: Which word is an adjective? | friendly | quickly | carefully | sadly
exercise: Which word is a verb? | jumped | jumpy | jump rope | jumper
exercise: In «We fish on Sundays», what word class is «fish»? | A verb | A noun, because a fish is an animal that lives in water | A pronoun | A determiner
exercise: Which group are all prepositions? | under, after, between | big, small, tall, wide, round | and, but, so | he, she, they
exercise: What does a pronoun do? | Stands in for a noun | Describes a verb | Joins two clauses together into one longer sentence | Names a particular place
test: «quickly's an adjective, it describes stuff, innit». What's the best reply? | It's an adverb: it tells how something is done | Yes, all describing words are adjectives | It's a noun, because it ends in -ly | It's a verb, because it's about moving fast
test: ★★ «The early bird got there early.» What word class is each «early»? | First an adjective, then an adverb | Both adjectives | Both adverbs | First a noun, then a verb
test: ★★ «friendly ends in -ly so it's an adverb, easy». Is it? | No, friendly describes a noun, so it's an adjective | Yes, every word ending in -ly is an adverb | Yes, but only when it is used in a friendly, kind sentence | No, it's a noun
test: ★★★ In «Mine is the red one, but hers is blue», which words are pronouns? | Mine and hers | red and blue | but and is | one and the
test: ★★★ «Before lunch, I tidied up. I'd never seen it before.» What word class is each «before»? | First a preposition, then an adverb | Both conjunctions | Both prepositions | First an adverb, then a noun that names a time
test: ★★★ How many nouns are in «Gran gave Tom a cake and a card on Friday»? | Five | Three | Four | Six
test: In a cartoon, Captain Grammar says «every word is born a noun or a verb and stays that way for ever». What's true? | A word's class depends on its job in the sentence | Every word has only one class for ever | Only nouns can change | Words have no classes, only spellings
test: «yeah nah, arvo isn't a real word». What's true? | It's Australian for afternoon, a real noun | It's a made-up sound with no meaning at all | It's a verb meaning to say yes | It's a swear word
test: What would happen if a sentence had no verb at all, like «The big red bus»? | It wouldn't be a full sentence | It would be a question | It would be a command | It would be more exciting
test: Mum shouts, «Proper noun, love, capital letter!» Which words need capitals in «tom went to paris on tuesday»? | Tom, Paris and Tuesday | Only tom, at the start | went and to | All of the words
test: «that film was well good». Which word is working like «very» here? | well | that | film | good
exam: Identify the word class of «run» in the sentence «I went for a run». | Noun | Verb | Adverb | Preposition, since it indicates the direction of the movement undertaken
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an adverb? | A word that modifies a verb, an adjective or another adverb | A word that names a person or place | A word that joins two clauses | A word that stands in for a noun
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the word «my» in «my dog». | It functions as a possessive determiner | It functions as a verb | It functions as a conjunction | It functions as a proper noun
exam: Ascertain which of the following words is an adjective notwithstanding its terminal «-ly». | lovely | slowly | quickly | happily
